WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:08.020
AddressSanitizer is a runtime tool for detecting many common memory-access defects while an instrumented program executes.

2
00:00:08.120 --> 00:00:20.440
ASan is widely used in C++ development because it turns classes of memory misuse that might otherwise appear intermittent into reports with source locations, stack traces, and useful lifetime clues.

3
00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:34.400
At a defensive level, ASan helps identify out-of-bounds reads and writes, use-after-free, some stack lifetime problems, and related cases where code accesses memory outside the valid object or lifetime.

4
00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:41.060
These are the kinds of defects that can cause crashes, corrupt state, or make security review much harder.

5
00:00:41.160 --> 00:00:49.160
ASan does not explain every design mistake automatically, but it can point to the moment where invalid access becomes observable.

6
00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:57.200
ASan works best when builds include useful debug information and tests exercise realistic code paths.

7
00:00:57.300 --> 00:01:00.980
Unit tests can catch focused boundary problems.

8
00:01:01.080 --> 00:01:08.200
Integration tests can expose ownership and lifetime issues that appear only when components interact.

9
00:01:08.300 --> 00:01:14.380
Longer-running tests may reveal teardown, shutdown, and error-path defects that short tests miss.

10
00:01:14.480 --> 00:01:20.000
The more meaningful the executed coverage, the more useful the sanitizer signal becomes.

11
00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:24.040
A report should be treated as a starting point for engineering analysis.

12
00:01:24.140 --> 00:01:37.020
The reported access may be the symptom, while the root cause may be a stale owner, mismatched length, invalidated iterator, incorrect container assumption, or API contract that allowed borrowed access to escape.

13
00:01:37.120 --> 00:01:45.200
Effective ASan use means following the evidence back to the ownership, lifetime, or bounds decision that made the invalid access possible.

14
00:01:45.480 --> 00:01:51.080
ASan is especially useful during modernization because it can make old assumptions visible.

15
00:01:51.180 --> 00:01:57.580
A legacy module may have worked for years because inputs followed a narrow pattern or because memory layout happened to be forgiving.

16
00:01:57.680 --> 00:02:03.360
Once tests broaden or dependencies change, the same code can reveal invalid access.

17
00:02:03.460 --> 00:02:09.380
Treat that visibility as an opportunity to strengthen interfaces, not just as a local defect to patch.
